WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.529
Good Morning John, it’s Friday...for the
last couple weeks, I’ve been thinking a

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:05.080
lot about boundaries.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.830
The boundary between public and private.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.200
The borders between nations.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.440
The thresholds of our lives.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.839
And the faultlines of ideology.

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:15.679
Interviewing President Obama was something
I could never regret, but being thrown into

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:19.290
the public eye has resulted in some people
saying nasty things about me, which I’m

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.590
actually not that used to.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:25.240
So I’ve been trying to look at it all from
outside of my own experience.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.920
My graduate degree is in environmental science,
so I often see my experiences through the

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.160
lens of ecology.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.530
Boundaries are exceptional places ecologically.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:40.550
The area where two biomes blend is called
an ecotone and they tend to be more diverse

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.950
and biologically productive than either biome
alone.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.920
Many species specialize in survival on that
edge where access to both biomes increases

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.680
the available resources

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.220
The edge, in other words, is a fantastic place.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.260
But it’s also a violent place.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.800
Predators have access to more prey, and prey
have to watch for more predators.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.570
That tension is, in nature, what drives increases
in productivity and diversity.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.580
The edge between the land and the sea has
always been and remains a source of great

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:17.560
biological productivity, but it has also given
rise to great cultural and economic productivity.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:22.520
The edge effect, it seems, exists outside
of ecology as well.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.460
There are personal edges, whether it’s the
finite moment a person becomes a parent.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.140
Or a more drawn out transition, like that
of child to adult.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:37.900
Difficult times, sure, but full of deeply
valuable personal growth.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.671
Just as ecological boundaries are massively
productive, tumultuous, and wonderful, so

00:01:42.671 --> 00:01:45.510
are boundaries between cultures.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.940
And in the last week I’ve tried to see the
political debate in this country that way.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:54.030
I’ve tried to imagine that the boundary
between ideologies is a productive yet tumultuous

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.790
place, and occasionally it is.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:01.150
But the benefits from the boundary come not
from a harsh, edge.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:05.600
Where the ocean meets a wall, the ecosystem
does not become more productive.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.450
The most productive ectones are those stretched
out over miles.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:13.440
The ocean eating the land, the land eating
the ocean in a never-ending burbling that

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.110
gives rise to beautiful but impenetrable wetlands.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:21.700
Instead of reaping the benefits of a gradient
between ideologies, I see people intentionally

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.810
poisoning it in order to protect their perceived
righteousness.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:31.620
Eventually, you have what ecologists call
a monoculture...one plant as far as the eye

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.620
can see.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:38.220
A desert of diversity that may be productive,
but is extremely unstable.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.330
Any outside influence can crash the entire
system.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:47.660
I do not want to live in a world of ideological
monocultures and I do not think that I do.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.720
But pretending like we do is in the best interests
of a few people who happen to have a lot of

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.720
power.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.340
So I do not propose dismantling the boundary...I
propose celebrating it.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.519
Ideological diversity is not a sign of a divided
nation, it’s a sign of a thoughtful nation.

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:06.480
And I’ve found the best way to really believe
this is just to simply stop listening to the

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.010
people telling us to hate each other.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:14.959
As wrong as you might think others are, you
are never always going to be right.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:19.489
Everyone will be stronger if viewpoints are
allowed to mix into a diversity of ideas,

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:20.720
and strategies.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.269
The ecological success of the edge comes from
this mixing, and I think it’s an important

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:28.210
part of the success of democracy as well.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.740
Yes, one side is going to feel safer to you,
it’s going to make more sense to you, but

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:37.840
to reach our full potential, we need the merge
that divide into a biome all its own, more

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.190
fertile, more diverse, and more productive
than we could ever be alone.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:44.110
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday

